  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>📐 Concrete Chemistry &amp; Failure Forensics</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>ASTM F1869 &amp; ASTM D4263</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      How to Test Concrete Moisture Before Applying Epoxy
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Moisture vapor transmission (MVT) is the #1 cause of catastrophic epoxy floor failure. Concrete behaves like a hard sponge: groundwater beneath your garage or basement slab continuously wicks upward and evaporates into the air. If you trap that vapor under an impermeable epoxy coating, hydrostatic pressure blisters the epoxy clean off the slab. Here is how to test before you coat.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Quick Screening Test</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">24-Hr Plastic Sheet (ASTM D4263)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Standard Epoxy Limit</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">&le; 3.0 lbs / 1,000 sq ft / 24h</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Quantitative Test</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Calcium Chloride (ASTM F1869)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The 24-Hour DIY Plastic Sheet Test (ASTM D4263)</h2>
    <p>
      This simple, zero-cost qualitative test will immediately tell you if your slab has a gross moisture vapor problem:
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-3 not-prose my-4">
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 1: Cut Heavy Plastic Squares</strong>
        Cut a 2-foot by 2-foot square of 6-mil clear polyethylene plastic sheeting.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 2: Airtight Perimeter Tape</strong>
        Place the plastic on a bare section of concrete (grind off any existing paint or grease). Seal all four edges 100% airtight to the concrete using heavy duct tape or moisture-resistant foil HVAC tape.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 3: Wait 24 to 48 Hours</strong>
        Leave the plastic undisturbed for at least 24 hours (preferably 48 hours).
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Step 4: Inspect for Condensation or Darkening</strong>
        Peel up the plastic. If you see water droplets on the underside of the sheet, or if the concrete beneath the plastic is distinctly darker in color than the surrounding dry slab, <strong>your slab has high moisture vapor transmission</strong>. Applying regular epoxy directly will fail.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The Professional Calcium Chloride Dome Test (ASTM F1869)</h2>
    <p>
      For guaranteed results, commercial coating contractors use an <strong>anhydrous calcium chloride test kit</strong> (cost: ~$25 on Amazon or at flooring suppliers):
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 font-bold mb-2">Moisture Vapor Emission Rate (MVER) Formula</div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 mb-2">A pre-weighed petri dish containing dry calcium chloride crystals is sealed under a transparent plastic dome for 60 to 72 hours. The salt absorbs moisture evaporating through the concrete. The dish is re-weighed on a gram scale.</p>
      <ul class="text-xs text-gray-600 space-y-1 list-disc pl-4 mb-0">
        <li><strong>Under 3.0 lbs:</strong> Safe for standard 100% solids epoxy basecoats.</li>
        <li><strong>3.0 to 5.0 lbs:</strong> Moderate moisture; requires a penetrating moisture-barrier primer.</li>
        <li><strong>Above 5.0 lbs (up to 25 lbs):</strong> Heavy moisture; requires a specialized 100% solids epoxy moisture vapor reduction membrane (such as Mapei Planiseal MVR or Koster VAP I 2000).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Elena Rostova, CBO"
    role="Certified Building Official & Material Takeoff Analyst"
    experience="18+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800"
    initials="ER"
    title="Why Moisture Testing in the Summer Can Fool You"
    note="Amateurs test their garage slab in July during a 3-week drought, get zero moisture under their plastic sheet, and coat the floor. Six months later in February and March, winter rains raise the local water table by 4 feet. Groundwater pushes upward against the underside of the unsealed slab, and large sheets of epoxy peel off like sunburned skin! Always check whether your slab was poured over an intact 10-mil poly vapor barrier. If your home was built before 1990, chances are high that the concrete was poured directly over dirt or gravel with zero vapor barrier. When in doubt, always spend the extra $150 on an epoxy moisture vapor barrier primer—it acts as an impenetrable underground dam."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Concrete Moisture Testing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Testing Over Sealed or Painted Concrete",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (False Dry Negative Readings)",
        description: "Taping test plastic over a slab that has leftover sealer, curing compound, or grease prevents vapor from escaping, giving a false reading that the concrete is dry.",
        solution: "Grind a 20x20 inch square down to bare, porous concrete with a wire wheel or angle grinder before taping down your plastic sheet or calcium chloride dome."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Testing Only One Spot in a 3-Car Garage",
        costImpact: "$2,400 (Localized Corner Delamination)",
        description: "Moisture levels vary dramatically across a slab. Low-lying rear corners near outdoor flower beds often have 3x higher vapor emissions than the front apron.",
        solution: "ASTM F1869 specifies running 3 separate calcium chloride test domes for the first 1,000 sq ft, and 1 additional test for every 1,000 sq ft thereafter."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Relying on Cheap Pinless Wood Moisture Meters",
        costImpact: "$600 (Inaccurate Electrical Resistance Data)",
        description: "Inexpensive handheld wood moisture meters only read electrical resistance in the top 1/8 inch of concrete, failing to measure the deep evaporative vapor pressure.",
        solution: "Use true calcium chloride test kits or in-situ relative humidity probes (ASTM F2170) drilled 40% into the slab thickness."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Concrete Moisture Testing Guide"
    reviews={[
      {
        author: "Darrell & Tina R.",
        location: "Seattle, WA",
        projectScope: "Basement Slab Moisture Testing",
        savingsClaim: "Saved $3,500 by Catching High MVT",
        feedback: "Did the plastic sheet test and after 24 hours the concrete was almost black with moisture droplets. Tested with calcium chloride and got 7.2 lbs MVER. Used a 100% solids epoxy moisture barrier primer before our metallic epoxy coat. Not a single bubble or blister after 18 months.",
        keyTakeaway: "Don't skip the moisture test even if the concrete looks bone dry to the eye; vapor is invisible until trapped."
      },
      {
        author: "Greg S.",
        location: "Omaha, NE",
        projectScope: "1970s Garage Floor Prep",
        savingsClaim: "Zero Hot Tire Peeling",
        feedback: "Learned our 1978 slab had no plastic barrier underneath. Put down a heavy coat of moisture mitigation primer as insurance. Parked my hot truck on it all summer and the coating is indestructible.",
        keyTakeaway: "Use blue foil HVAC tape to seal test plastic; regular blue painter's tape will leak vapor at the edges."
      }
    ]}
  />

